### What does this question ask?

The interviewer wants you to find the longest string in a list using the Stream API. This tests max, Comparator.comparingInt, Optional handling, and tie-breaking.

### How do you find the longest string?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.*;

public class LongestString {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> codes = List.of(
            "SAV", "SAV2001", "HDFC-SAVINGS-2001", "CUR", "FD2024");

        Optional<String> longest = codes.stream()
            .max(Comparator.comparingInt(String::length));

        longest.ifPresent(s ->
            System.out.println("Longest: " + s + " (" + s.length() + " chars)"));
    }
}
```

```text
Longest: HDFC-SAVINGS-2001 (17 chars)
```

### Why use comparingInt instead of comparing?

| Method | Extractor type | Boxing |
|---|---|---|
| comparingInt(String::length) | ToIntFunction | No boxing |
| comparing(s -> s.length()) | Function returning Integer | Auto-boxes each length |

```java
// Preferred: no boxing
Comparator.comparingInt(String::length)

// Works but boxes each length to Integer
Comparator.comparing(String::length)
```

comparingInt is slightly more efficient and communicates that the key is a primitive.

### How is max different from sort-then-last?

```java
// max: single pass O(n), clear intent
Optional<String> maxApproach = codes.stream()
    .max(Comparator.comparingInt(String::length));

// sort-then-last: O(n log n), buffers all elements
String sortApproach = codes.stream()
    .sorted(Comparator.comparingInt(String::length))
    .reduce((first, second) -> second)
    .orElse("");
```

max is both faster and more readable when you only need the extreme value.

### How do you handle ties?

If several strings share the maximum length, max returns one arbitrary winner.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.*;

public class LongestWithTieBreak {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> codes = List.of("ABCDEF", "ZYXWVU", "MNOPQR");

        // Without tie-breaking: arbitrary among length-6 strings
        Optional<String> arbitrary = codes.stream()
            .max(Comparator.comparingInt(String::length));

        // With tie-breaking: alphabetically first among equal-length
        Optional<String> deterministic = codes.stream()
            .max(Comparator.comparingInt(String::length)
                .thenComparing(Comparator.naturalOrder()));

        System.out.println("Arbitrary: " + arbitrary.orElse(""));
        System.out.println("Deterministic: " + deterministic.orElse(""));
    }
}
```

```text
Arbitrary: MNOPQR
Deterministic: ZYXWVU
```

### How do you find the shortest string?

Replace max with min.

```java
Optional<String> shortest = codes.stream()
    .min(Comparator.comparingInt(String::length));
```

### What are the edge cases?

```java
// Empty list: returns Optional.empty
Optional<String> empty = List.<String>of().stream()
    .max(Comparator.comparingInt(String::length));
System.out.println(empty.isPresent());  // false

// Single element: that element is both max and min
Optional<String> single = List.of("SAV").stream()
    .max(Comparator.comparingInt(String::length));
System.out.println(single.get());  // SAV
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: not filtering null elements.

```java
// WRONG: NPE because comparingInt calls length() on null
List<String> withNull = Arrays.asList("SAV", null, "HDFC");
Optional<String> wrong = withNull.stream()
    .max(Comparator.comparingInt(String::length));  // NPE!

// CORRECT: filter nulls first
Optional<String> correct = withNull.stream()
    .filter(Objects::nonNull)
    .max(Comparator.comparingInt(String::length));
```

Mistake: using sorted().findFirst() instead of min.

```java
// WRONG: sorts the entire stream just to find the minimum
Optional<String> expensive = codes.stream()
    .sorted(Comparator.comparingInt(String::length))
    .findFirst();

// CORRECT: min is a single pass
Optional<String> efficient = codes.stream()
    .min(Comparator.comparingInt(String::length));
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you find all strings of maximum length?" First find the max length, then filter all strings matching it.
- "How do you find the longest string per group?" Use groupingBy with collectingAndThen(maxBy, Optional::get).
- "What is the time complexity?" O(n) for max, versus O(n log n) for sorting.
